    <h3>Website Micro-markup for Search Engines</h3>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Open Graph.</b> This option enables automatic Open Graph markup for attractive link previews on social networks and messaging apps. The system automatically populates the title, description, and image, and ensures the correct canonical URL is used to prevent duplicate previews;</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Twitter Card.</b> This option enables automatic Twitter Card (X Card) markup so that page links display attractively on X—featuring a title, description, large image, and (if applicable) an embedded player. The system automatically populates the necessary data and ensures the correct canonical URL is used to prevent duplicate previews;</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Schema.org.</b> This option enables automatic Schema.org markup, allowing search engines to generate rich snippets in search results—such as displaying prices and availability for products, dates and authors for articles, or quick answers for FAQs. The system automatically maps page fields (title, description, image, price, etc.) to JSON-LD, selects the appropriate entity type (Product, Article, etc.), and ensures the data matches what is actually displayed to the user;</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>VKontakte.</b> This option enables automatic VKontakte markup for attractive link previews in the VK feed—featuring a title, brief description, and cover image. You don’t need to write the code manually—the system will automatically pull the data from the page card fields.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Application signature</h3>
    <p>Parameters determine how information is specified regarding the system or tool used to create the page:</p>
